{"ts": 1789783208.763904, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "These diagnostics are genuinely informative.\n\n**Sign coherence.** Flipping signs collapses the spec (0.991 \u2192 0.381, 0.161, 0.457). Under pure noise, a sign flip of one of three components would give roughly a symmetric \u00b1 spread around zero, not a clean monotone ordering with one dominant orientation. The chosen orientation is meaningfully preferred.\n\n**The null combinations behave like a null.** Features I picked from the *middle* of the ranking ({3,23,30} and {26,31,34}) give 0.041 and 0.002 \u2014 essentially zero, not a value inflated by three-feature averaging. This is an important control: it confirms that combining three features does not mechanically manufacture Sharpe. So the ~0.99 on my top spec isn't a pure artifact of the combination step.\n\n**But the diversification math tells the cautionary half.** f38 alone is 0.714. Adding two more features of comparable strength with low correlation should lift Sharpe by up to \u221a3 \u2248 1.73\u00d7 if all three carried equal independent signal \u2014 that would be ~1.2. I'm getting 0.991, i.e. roughly 1.39\u00d7. That's consistent with f29 and f25 carrying *some* real but weaker signal, part of their apparent standalone strength being selection noise.\n\nLet me estimate how much of the top spec is selection-inflated by checking a couple of specs built on features chosen for *coherence* rather than rank."}
