{"ts": 1789783225.489912, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "This is the decisive result. Adding an *arbitrary* third feature to the {38, 29} core yields 0.80\u20130.87 almost regardless of which feature I add. The pair alone is 0.879. So the third slot contributes essentially **nothing** systematic \u2014 every candidate lands in the same band, and my \"best\" third feature (\u221225, at 0.991) is simply the luckiest draw from that band, sitting about 0.12 above the typical 0.87.\n\nThat 0.12 is the selection premium, and I should expect it to vanish out of sample. The honest read is that the real structure here is the {38, 29} pair, worth ~0.88 in sample, and even that is the top pair out of ~3,100 signed pairs.\n\nI'll submit the three-feature spec since it's my best in-sample point, but I will not predict its in-sample value. Let me shrink deliberately.\n\n**Estimating the out-of-sample mean.** Three layers of shrinkage:\n1. f38 and f29 were selected as the top 2 of 40 singles. With SE \u2248 0.22, the winner's-curse inflation on a max-of-40 is roughly 2\u03c3 \u2248 0.4 \u2014 but only on the portion that's noise. Their combined 0.879 is likely worth ~0.55\u20130.65 truly.\n2. The third feature adds ~0 systematically; its apparent +0.11 is selection. Drop it.\n3. The whole spec was chosen as the max over my search path, adding further optimistic bias.\n\nThat points to a central estimate near **0.55**. The search-adjusted bar of 1.018 corroborates this: the environment is telling me that ~1.0 in-sample is what noise alone produces here, and my 0.991 does not clear it. A spec that fails to beat the noise benchmark should be predicted well below its in-sample value \u2014 plausibly near zero. I'll set the mean modestly above zero because the sign-coherence and null-control evidence do indicate genuine signal in f38/f29, but not much above.\n\n**Estimating the uncertainty.** Pure sampling error on a fresh sample is ~0.22. But my dominant uncertainty is *how much* of the in-sample result is selection \u2014 I estimated that correction roughly, and it could easily be off by 0.3 in either direction. Combining, an SD of **0.45** is appropriate. This honestly admits the outcome could be near zero, or could be ~1.0 if the signal is more real than I credit."}
