{"ts": 1789783264.74596, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "## Summary\n\n**Submitted specification:** long f38, long f29, short f25 \u2014 in-sample Sharpe **0.991**.\n\n**Predicted OOS Sharpe: 0.50 \u00b1 0.45.**\n\n### How I got there\n\nI scanned all 40 features individually, then built up pairs and triples from the strongest candidates. The in-sample maximum reached 0.991.\n\n### Why I predict roughly half the in-sample number\n\nThe critical context is scale. With n=5000 daily observations, the standard error on an annualized Sharpe is \u221a(252/5000) \u2248 0.22. The search space is ~39,500 signed 3-feature combinations, so the best-of-space under *pure noise* is around 0.9. The reported bar of 1.018 matches that arithmetic \u2014 and my best spec does not clear it.\n\nThree diagnostics separated signal from selection:\n\n- **Sign coherence (supports real signal):** flipping signs collapsed the spec from 0.991 to 0.16\u20130.46. Noise wouldn't produce such a clean preferred orientation.\n- **Null controls (supports real signal):** mid-ranked feature triples like {3,23,30} and {26,31,34} scored 0.041 and 0.002 \u2014 confirming that merely averaging three features doesn't mechanically manufacture Sharpe.\n- **Third-slot substitution test (the damning one):** I swapped six different features into the third slot of the {38,29} core. Every one landed at 0.80\u20130.87, versus 0.879 for the pair alone. The third feature contributes nothing systematic \u2014 my \"winning\" \u2212f25 is just the luckiest draw from that band, and its apparent +0.12 edge is pure selection premium that should evaporate out of sample.\n\nSo the genuine structure is the {38,29} pair (~0.88 in-sample), itself the winner of ~3,100 signed pairs and thus inflated by winner's curse. Correcting for selection at the single-feature stage, the third-slot stage, and the overall-search stage brings me to ~0.5.\n\nI deliberately **stopped searching** rather than grinding toward a higher number. An exhaustive sweep would almost certainly surface something at 1.1\u20131.2, but that figure would be *less* predictive out of sample, not more \u2014 it would just be a better fit to noise. The remaining calls went to diagnostics instead of maximization.\n\nThe wide SD of 0.45 reflects that my dominant uncertainty isn't sampling error (~0.22) but the size of my own selection correction, which is a rough estimate. The interval honestly spans \"essentially zero\" through \"~1.0.\""}
